# Comments (Unity style guide)

From [Unity's C# style guide](https://unity.com/resources/c-sharp-style-guide-unity-6). Prefer clear names and small methods first; comment what code cannot say on its own.

## Dos and don'ts

| Don't | Do instead |
|-------|------------|
| Comment to **excuse unclear code** | **Rename, extract a method, or simplify** so the code explains itself |
| Restate what the next line obviously does | Explain **why** — constraints, order, Unity lifecycle, ticket link |
| Leave **commented-out** code in place | Delete it; git history retains old versions |
| Let comments **rot** when behaviour changes | Update or remove comments in the **same PR** as the code |
| Narrate authorship or dates in-line | Use **git blame** and commit messages |

**Don't add comments to replace bad code.** If you need a comment to explain what a block does, refactor until the names and structure are clear; then comment only what is still non-obvious (usually *why*).

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — comment patches unclear code
// Check if player is dead and destroy enemies
if (hp <= 0) { … }

// ✅ GOOD — code speaks; comment only leftover why
if (m_hp <= 0)
{
    EnterDeathState();
}
// Death state suppresses respawn until revive UI completes (GD-88)
```

## When to comment

- **Non-obvious intent** — business rules, Unity quirks, performance tradeoffs.
- **Public API** — `///` XML on types and members other systems consume.
- **Temporary gaps** — `TODO` / `FIXME` with ticket; remove when done.

## XML documentation

Use `///` summary (and param/returns when helpful) for `public` / `protected` surfaces in shared assemblies, extension utilities, and generated-doc targets. Skip XML noise on private helpers.

## TODO / FIXME

```csharp
// TODO(GD-42): Replace FindObjectOfType with injected reference when bootstrap lands
```

One line, actionable, traceable.

**Unity notes** (when not obvious): script execution order, serialization limits, editor-only code paths.

Match surrounding comment style in legacy files unless you are refactoring that area.
